</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our foundational model mainly employs the recently proposed Prior-Data Fitted Networks (PFNs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al. 2021</a>)</cite> as the basic architecture, which is based on a Transformer encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al. 2017</a>)</cite>. We utilize linear layers to encode trajectory and query points, along with sampled preferences, as contextual inputs for the foundation model. Following the methods outlined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hollmann et al. 2022</a>)</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, we set a maximum dimension <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> for encoding the inputs, which are applicable to both feature and objective dimensions. We zero-pad the sampled dimension <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and linearly scale the dimension by a factor of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/k</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that the magnitudes of the input encodings are consistent across different dimensions. Since different query points should not depend on one another, an attention mask is implemented to allow each trajectory point can only attend to each other, while the query points can only attend to the trajectory points. To maintain the permutation invariance of the input dataset, the positional encodings are removed and the input points are fed to the model as the sum of their encoded embeddings. During training, we maintain a maximum sample length of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> trajectory points and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-n</annotation></semantics></math> query points, as used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. We sample each <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,...,N-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,...,N-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> with the weight of <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(N-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(N-n)</annotation></semantics></math> to simulate varying trajectory sizes during the optimization process, while ensuring that each trajectory size has approximately the same number of query points for training. To model the continuous distribution for prediction, the regression head of PFNs discretizes the continuous distribution into a piecewise constant distribution, referred to as the Riemann distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. This helps transform the model training from a regression task to a classification problem. The boundaries of each discrete interval are estimated by sampling a large synthetic dataset from the same synthetic prior, ensuring that each interval maintains equal probability in the prior data. Additionally, the last bar on each side is replaced with a suitably scaled half-normal distribution to enhance training stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During the pre-training phase, we extensively sample from a data generation mechanism to create a large and diverse set of synthetic data. Initially, we sample the number of features <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of objectives <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and the trajectory length <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> from 1 up to their predetermined maximum values. We then sample <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> independent latent functions from a Gaussian Process (GP) prior with zero mean and the radial basis function (RBF) kernel:</p>
<table id="A3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\sim\mathcal{G}\mathcal{P}(0,k_{\textnormal{RBF}}(\cdot,\cdot)),\quad k_{\textnormal{RBF}}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\exp(-\frac{\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}\|^{2}}{2l^{2}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>RBF</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>RBF</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\sim\mathcal{G}\mathcal{P}(0,k_{\textnormal{RBF}}(\cdot,\cdot)),\quad k_{\textnormal{RBF}}(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}\exp(-\frac{\|\boldsymbol{x}-\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}\|^{2}}{2l^{2}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> represent the output scale and length scale kernel parameters, respectively. We also sample the length scale parameter for each feature dimension during the latent function sampling, along with an random observation noise to enhance function diversity. In the third step, we randomly sample input features from the <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional unit cube, and then evaluate the sampled latent functions to generate the corresponding observations, resulting in <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> trajectory samples and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N-n</annotation></semantics></math> query samples. After that, we randomly sample the artificial preferences from the simplex <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{\lambda}\|_{1}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{\lambda}\|_{1}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to derive the aggregation targets <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> given query inputs and preferences. The aggregation targets are then masked to formulate a supervised prediction problem, enabling the model to learn to predict the preference-wise aggregated posterior distribution <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(g|\boldsymbol{x},D_{n};\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>𝝀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}(g|\boldsymbol{x},D_{n};\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>. We continuously synthesize data through extensive sampling and optimize the model parameters to minimize the cross-entropy loss function defined in Equation (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.4 Training Details ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ FoMEMO: Towards Foundation Models for Expensive Multi-objective Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), which is equivalent to the expected KL divergence between the true posterior distribution and its approximation, up to an additive constant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Müller et al. 2021</a>)</cite>. The detailed information and hyperparameter settings regarding the foundational model and the pre-training process can be found in Table <a href="#A3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ C.1 Details of Foundation Model and Pre-training ‣ Appendix C Model and Algorithm Details ‣ FoMEMO: Towards Foundation Models for Expensive Multi-objective Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Visualization of Aggregated Posteriors</h3>

<figure id="A3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03244v1/toy_aggregated_posteriors.png" id="A3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:615/405;" width="615" height="405" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Details of the normalized objective functions for the ToyRobust problem, the aggregated posteriors (green solid lines with shaded regions) conditioned on 5 observations and 5 preferences, and the corresponding UCB acquisition functions (red solid lines).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the convenience of visualization, we use a simple one-dimensional ToyRobust problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Daulton et al. 2022</a>)</cite> as an example to demonstrate that the pre-trained foundation model can effectively learn to infer the aggregated posteriors on unknown problems, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#A3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ C.2 Visualization of Aggregated Posteriors ‣ Appendix C Model and Algorithm Details ‣ FoMEMO: Towards Foundation Models for Expensive Multi-objective Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The results show that the foundation model provides reliable predictions given the evaluated trajectory of the out-of-distribution problem without any model training and update, allows the model to generalize beyond the synthetic training data. The subproblems defined by adjacent preferences exhibit similar characteristics in terms of the scalarization functions, the predicted mean captures this similarity effectively. Furthermore, the prediction provides a reliable measure of calibration uncertainty, enabling the acquisition function such as upper confidence bound (UCB) shown in the figure to effectively balance exploration and exploitation during the search for candidate solutions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Derivation of UHVI Acquisition Function</h3>

<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Definitions and preliminaries.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The derivation mainly depends on two types of definitions on Tchebycheff scalarizations and the theorem on hypervolume scalarization. We consider the the following multi-objective optimization problem (MOP):</p>
<table id="A3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}}\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x})=(f_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}),f_{2}(\boldsymbol{x}),\cdots,f_{m}(\boldsymbol{x}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}}\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x})=(f_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}),f_{2}(\boldsymbol{x}),\cdots,f_{m}(\boldsymbol{x})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We denote <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated samples, where each <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{d})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{d})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the feature vector in the decision space <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}=(y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{m})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}=(y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{m})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the observation vector of the true objective function <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{f}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}^{*}=(z_{1}^{*},\cdots,z_{m}^{*})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}^{*}=(z_{1}^{*},\cdots,z_{m}^{*})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as the ideal point (lower bound) in the objective space. We have the Tchebycheff scalarization function:</p>
<table id="A3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\text{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x})=\max_{1\leq j\leq m}\{\lambda_{j}(y_{j}(\boldsymbol{x})-z_{j}^{*})\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝝀</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\text{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x})=\max_{1\leq j\leq m}\{\lambda_{j}(y_{j}(\boldsymbol{x})-z_{j}^{*})\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\ldots,\lambda_{m})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\ldots,\lambda_{m})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a preference sampled from the simplex <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{\lambda}\|_{1}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{\lambda}\|_{1}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can identify each Pareto optimal solution by minimizing <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\text{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝝀</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\text{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math> with a specific but unknown preference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choo and Atkins 1983</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Tchebycheff scalarization function can be transformed in another formulation with respect to a set of reference
vectors <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}=(w_{1},w_{2},\ldots,w_{m})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}=(w_{1},w_{2},\ldots,w_{m})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{w}\|_{2}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒘</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{w}\|_{2}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\text{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x})=\max_{1\leq j\leq m}\{(y_{j}(\boldsymbol{x})-z_{j}^{*})/w_{j}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\text{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x})=\max_{1\leq j\leq m}\{(y_{j}(\boldsymbol{x})-z_{j}^{*})/w_{j}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Leveraging Equation (<a href="#A3.E12" title="In Definitions and preliminaries. ‣ C.3 Derivation of UHVI Acquisition Function ‣ Appendix C Model and Algorithm Details ‣ FoMEMO: Towards Foundation Models for Expensive Multi-objective Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), we have the following theorem, which will help us approximate the hypervolume indicator and further derive UHVI acquisition function.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hypervolume Scalarization)<span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated samples, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}^{*}=(z_{1}^{*},\cdots,z_{m}^{*})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}^{*}=(z_{1}^{*},\cdots,z_{m}^{*})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal point, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{r}=(r_{1},\cdots,r_{m})^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{r}=(r_{1},\cdots,r_{m})^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pre-defined reference point. The hypervolume of the evaluated samples can be expressed as an expectation of Tchebycheff scalarization function over a set of uniformly sampled reference vectors <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{w}\|_{2}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{\boldsymbol{w}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{m}\mid\|\boldsymbol{w}\|_{2}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng and Zhang 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Golovin 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2023</a>)</cite>:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{HV}=\prod_{j=1}^{m}(r_{j}-z_{j}^{*})-c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>HV</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{HV}=\prod_{j=1}^{m}(r_{j}-z_{j}^{*})-c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{m}=\frac{\pi^{m/2}}{2^{m}\Gamma(m/2+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{m}=\frac{\pi^{m/2}}{2^{m}\Gamma(m/2+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant that depends only on <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Utilizing the hypervolume scalarization and Equation (<a href="#A3.E11" title="In Definitions and preliminaries. ‣ C.3 Derivation of UHVI Acquisition Function ‣ Appendix C Model and Algorithm Details ‣ FoMEMO: Towards Foundation Models for Expensive Multi-objective Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), we can naturally derive the utility of UHVI acquisition function:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hypervolume Improvment with Uncertainty)<span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the the distribution of the aggregation target <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\cdot)=-s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝛌</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝛌</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\cdot)=-s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on evaluated samples <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the utility of hypervolume improvement in the normalized objective space at any query point <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> can be estimated by the following expectation:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\textnormal{UHVI}}(\boldsymbol{x})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-\textnormal{UCB}(g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}))]^{m}\}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>UHVI</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>UCB</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\textnormal{UHVI}}(\boldsymbol{x})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-\textnormal{UCB}(g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}))]^{m}\}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmlemma1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{m}=\frac{\pi^{m/2}}{2^{m}\Gamma(m/2+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{m}=\frac{\pi^{m/2}}{2^{m}\Gamma(m/2+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant that depends only on <math id="Thmlemma1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlemma1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{m}\frac{1}{\lambda_{j}^{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝛌</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{m}\frac{1}{\lambda_{j}^{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a transformation constant that depends only on <math id="Thmlemma1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlemma1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{UCB}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>UCB</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{UCB}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the upper confidence bound (UCB) metric.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">From Equation (<a href="#A3.E13" title="In Theorem 1 (Hypervolume Scalarization). ‣ Definitions and preliminaries. ‣ C.3 Derivation of UHVI Acquisition Function ‣ Appendix C Model and Algorithm Details ‣ FoMEMO: Towards Foundation Models for Expensive Multi-objective Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), given an updated evaluated samples <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{n+1}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{n+1}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with newly added sample <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1},\boldsymbol{y}_{n+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1},\boldsymbol{y}_{n+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the hypervolume can be represented as:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{HV}^{\prime}=\prod_{j=1}^{m}(r_{j}-z_{j}^{*})-c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n+1}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mtext>HV</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{HV}^{\prime}=\prod_{j=1}^{m}(r_{j}-z_{j}^{*})-c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n+1}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">which induces the hypervolume improvement:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A3.E16X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E16X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textnormal{HVI}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>HVI</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textnormal{HVI}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E16X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\textnormal{HV}^{\prime}-\textnormal{HV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>HV</mtext><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mtext>HV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\textnormal{HV}^{\prime}-\textnormal{HV}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E16Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E16Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[\min_{1\leq i\leq n+1}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[\min_{1\leq i\leq n+1}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E16Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E16Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[\min\{\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i}),s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})\}]^{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}\{[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[\min\{\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i}),s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})\}]^{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E16Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E16Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}[\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})]^{m}\}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝒘</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{w}\sim S}[\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[s_{\boldsymbol{w}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})]^{m}\}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Given the transformation constant <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{m}\frac{1}{\lambda_{j}^{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}=\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{m}\frac{1}{\lambda_{j}^{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, each reference vector <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely represented by the preference <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> by using <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}=1/({\lambda_{j}c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}=1/({\lambda_{j}c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, the hypervolume improvement can be represented as:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{HVI}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})]^{m}\}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>HVI</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝝀</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝝀</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{HVI}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})]^{m}-[s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})]^{m}\}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.4.1" class="ltx_text">Our model predict the distribution of the aggregation target <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})=-s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>𝝀</mi><mtext>tch</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})=-s_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}^{\textnormal{tch}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> given each <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure that objectives of varying magnitudes share the same scale, we normalize the objectives to the range of [0,1] during the optimization process. Consequently, the hypervolume improvement in the normalized objective space can be reformulated as follows:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{HVI}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})]^{m}\}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>HVI</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{HVI}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})]^{m}\}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.5.1" class="ltx_text">Here the aggregation target <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is constrained to be non-positive after the objective normalization (see Figure <a href="#A3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ C.2 Visualization of Aggregated Posteriors ‣ Appendix C Model and Algorithm Details ‣ FoMEMO: Towards Foundation Models for Expensive Multi-objective Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for an example). Since we wish to maximize the aggregation target at the query point <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for maximizing hypervolume improvement, we replace the prediction <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textnormal{UCB}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>UCB</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textnormal{UCB}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> metric as an upper bound estimation, which introduces the calibrated uncertainty provided by our model for searching the candidates with a good balance of exploration and exploitation:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\textnormal{HVI}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-\textnormal{UCB}(g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}))]^{m}\}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mtext>HVI</mtext><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>UCB</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\textnormal{HVI}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-\textnormal{UCB}(g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}))]^{m}\}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.6.1" class="ltx_text">We can then derive the utility of UHVI acquisition function as <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary:</span></p>
<table id="A3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\textnormal{UHVI}}(\boldsymbol{x})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-\textnormal{UCB}(g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}))]^{m}\}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>UHVI</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>UCB</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝀</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\textnormal{UHVI}}(\boldsymbol{x})=c_{m}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}\sim\Delta}[(c_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}})^{m}\max\{0,[\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{-g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i})\}]^{m}-[-\textnormal{UCB}(g_{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}(\boldsymbol{x}))]^{m}\}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.7.1" class="ltx_text">which leads to our claim in the lemma.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, maximizing <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\textnormal{UHVI}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>UHVI</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\textnormal{UHVI}}</annotation></semantics></math> utility is equivalent to maximizing the predicted upper bounds of aggregation targets across all preference simultaneously. We find that our utility exhibits similar expression to that presented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. 2024b</a>)</cite>. However, the key difference is that the formulation of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. 2024b</a>)</cite> is based on the Gaussian process (GP) posterior for each objective, whereas our acquisition function is directly derived from infinite preference-wise aggregated posteriors, which is intractable for traditional GP training and updates.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
